.\" man page for libvirt-backup
.TH LIBVIRT-BACKUP 1 "2026-11-07" "0.1.5" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
libvirt-backup \- push backup and restore tool for libvirt/QEMU virtual machines
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B libvirt-backup backup
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B libvirt-backup restore
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B libvirt-backup forget
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B libvirt-backup check
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B libvirt-backup init
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B libvirt-backup
backs up every running domain of a libvirt host entirely through the
libvirt API (guest-agent fsFreeze,
.BR backupBegin ,
blockjob monitoring, dumpxml).
Artefacts per VM are a domain backup XML, one image per disk, the live
domain XML, a log and the selection state file
.IR <vm> -state.json.
.PP
Images are either written as standalone qcow2/raw files into a backup
directory, or flattened to raw and streamed into a deduplicated,
encrypted rustic/restic repository (e.g. S3).
.PP
Before every VM the backup order is recomputed and the running VM whose
last successful backup lies furthest back is picked next
(never-backed-up VMs first), so an interrupted long run continues where
it stopped instead of starving the alphabetically last VMs, and a
live-migrated VM keeps its real backup history. The last success comes
from the repository itself in repository mode (shared across hosts) and
from
.I <vm>-state.json
in directory mode.
.I backoff
(config key, or
.IR \-\-backoff )
sets the minimum time between two backups of the same VM;
.I \-D
selections bypass it. After more than 2 consecutive failures the
administrator is notified and the VM is only retried once the backoff
period has passed since the last error; any success resets the counter.
The
.B restore
subcommand places one VM's images and XMLs into a destination directory
and runs on a rescue system without libvirt.
.B forget
applies the retention policy of the
.B [retention]
config section to the repository;
.B check
verifies repository integrity.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B backup
back up all (or the selected) running domains
.TP
.B restore
place one VM's backup into a destination directory
(\fB\-\-list\fR shows available backups)
.TP
.B forget
apply retention (repository mode); use \fB\-\-dry-run\fR first
.TP
.B check
verify images (directory mode) or repository integrity (repo mode)
.TP
.B init
create an empty rustic/restic repository (once, before the first backup)
.SH OPTIONS
Run
.BI "libvirt-backup " subcommand " \-\-help"
for the full option list of each subcommand.
Common options:
.BR \-d / \-\-dest ", " \-D / \-\-domain ", " \-\-config ", "
.BR \-\-dry-run ", " \-\-verify .
.PP
Locks are per host and per VM: the run lock
.RI ( .libvirt-backup-<host> .lock )
lets one run per host, a second concurrent
.B backup
warns and skips (exit 0), and each VM backup holds that VM's lock
.RI ( .libvirt-backup-vm-<vm> .lock ),
which spans hosts on a shared backup directory - a VM live-migrated
while still being backed up is skipped by the new host instead of
racing for the same artefacts.
.I \-\-force
proceeds despite a lock left by a stuck run (last resort).
.SH CONFIGURATION
TOML file, searched at
.IR /etc/libvirt-backup/libvirt-backup.toml ", "
.IR /etc/libvirt-backup.toml " (legacy), "
.IR ~/.config/libvirt-backup.toml ;
precedence is command line > config file > defaults.
Sections:
.BR [backup] ", " [domains.NAME-or-glob] ", " [repo] ", " [retention] .
Ready-made templates are installed as
.IR /etc/libvirt-backup/libvirt-backup.toml.directory-mode
and
.IR .repository-mode ;
the systemd units stay skipped until
.IR /etc/libvirt-backup/libvirt-backup.toml
exists.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/bin/libvirt-backup
the tool
.TP
.I /etc/libvirt-backup/
configuration directory (units, templates)
.TP
.I /var/lib/libvirt/backups
default backup directory
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR virsh (1),
.BR qemu-img (1),
.BR rustic (1),
restic
.SH AUTHOR
Thomas Kotzian <thomas.kotzian@lausa.at>
